// batch ab: acostanu, sedan, littlebighorn, isandlwana, angamos, ulundi
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "tríplice aliança": "The alliance of Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay against Paraguay, signed in 1865. Hence the name War of the Triple Alliance, as the Paraguayans call the war.",
  "arroio": "A creek. On the plains of Paraguay, creeks with muddy banks formed natural obstacles for infantry and cavalry.",
  "chassepot": "A French breech-loading rifle that reached farther than the Prussian needle gun. It was one of the few advantages of the French infantry in 1870.",
  "fuzil de agulha": "The Prussian Dreyse breech-loading rifle: the soldier could reload lying down. It was older and had a shorter range than the French chassepot.",
  "krupp": "A German arms maker. Its breech-loading steel cannons were more accurate and longer-ranged than the French bronze cannons.",
  "bolsão": "An area surrounded on all sides, from which an army can neither escape nor receive supplies.",
  "confederação do norte da alemanha": "A union of 22 German states led by Prussia, created in 1867. In 1870, the southern states (such as Bavaria) joined it in the war against France.",
  "infantaria de marinha": "Navy soldiers trained to fight on land. The French used them to defend Bazeilles.",
  "caçadores da áfrica": "The Chasseurs d'Afrique, French cavalry recruited in Algeria and famous for rapid charges.",
  "lakota": "The people known as the Sioux. The Lakota are divided into several groups, among them the Hunkpapa (Sitting Bull's) and the Oglala (Crazy Horse's).",
  "colinas negras": "A mountainous region of South Dakota, sacred to the Lakota. The 1868 treaty guaranteed it to them, until gold was discovered in 1874.",
  "batedores": "Soldiers or guides who go ahead of the army to find the enemy and the way. At the Little Bighorn, Custer's scouts were Crow and Arikara.",
  "dança do sol": "A sacred ceremony of the plains, held in summer. Sitting Bull had his vision of victory during a Sun Dance.",
  "companhia": "A military unit of at most a hundred men. A company of the 7th Cavalry had, in practice, well under that, because of casualties and illness.",
  "comboio de mulas": "A line of mules loaded with ammunition and food that accompanied the column. It was slow, and Custer left it behind.",
  "chifres do búfalo": "The Zulu attack formation: the chest holds the enemy from the front, the two horns run along the sides to encircle him, and the loins stay behind as a reserve. It was created or perfected by Shaka, the Zulu king in the 1820s.",
  "impi": "A Zulu army, or a regiment of it. The warriors were organized in regiments by age and trained from youth.",
  "assegai": "The Zulus' short spear, for throwing or stabbing. Shaka popularized a broad-bladed version, suited to close combat.",
  "martini-henry": "A British single-shot, breech-loading rifle. It was powerful, but it heated up and jammed with continuous use.",
  "linha de fogo": "Soldiers side by side, in open order, firing at the same time. It covers a wide front, but grows weak if stretched too far.",
  "donga": "A dry gully cut by rain into the African ground. It served as a natural trench.",
  "foguetes": "War rockets launched from a metal trough. The British used the Hale system: they frightened and made noise, but hit poorly.",
  "cruz vitória": "The highest British military decoration, created in 1856.",
  "metralhadora gatling": "A weapon with several rotating barrels, worked by a hand crank, that fired hundreds of rounds a minute. It was the most widely used machine gun of the 19th century.",
  "encouraçado": "A warship protected by iron plates, from 1860 on. An ordinary cannonball barely penetrated it.",
  "monitor": "A low, armored warship with one or two revolving gun turrets. It was hard to hit, but sailed poorly on the open sea.",
  "torre blindada": "A revolving iron dome with the guns inside. It allows firing in any direction without turning the ship.",
  "corveta": "A small, fast warship, smaller than a frigate. It was used for patrolling and escort.",
  "salitre": "A mineral used as fertilizer and in gunpowder. In the 1870s, the Atacama Desert was the world's largest producer, and the dispute over it gave rise to the War of the Pacific.",
  "guerra do pacífico": "The war of 1879 to 1884 in which Chile fought Peru and Bolivia for control of the Atacama Desert and its saltpeter. Chile won and kept the coast that had been Bolivian and part of southern Peru."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  acostanu: {
    war: "Paraguayan War, or War of the Triple Alliance (1864–1870)",
    hook: "On a field of dry grass, a rear guard of boys and old men faced an army of 20,000 for eight hours.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The war began in December 1864, when Marshal Francisco Solano López, dictator of Paraguay, ordered the invasion of Mato Grosso and, afterward, of Corrientes, in Argentina. In 1865, Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay united in the [[Triple Alliance|tríplice aliança]] against him. Paraguay was small, but it had an army that was large and well trained for the time, and the war, which everyone thought would be short, lasted more than five years.",
        "At the end of 1868, López's army was nearly destroyed in the battles of December, and he escaped from Lomas Valentinas. Asunción, the capital, was occupied by the allies in early 1869. The Marquis of Caxias, the Brazilian commander, thought the war was militarily over and left the command in February. Emperor Pedro II, however, wanted López captured or expelled, and sent in his place his son-in-law, Gastão de Orléans, the Count of Eu.",
        { h: "The road to the field" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1 May 1869", "The Count of Eu begins the march into the interior of Paraguay."],
          ["12 August 1869", "The allies win at Peribebuí."],
          ["15 August", "They take Caacupé. López had already moved on to Caraguatay, leaving Caballero to protect the rear."],
          ["16 August", "The Paraguayan column is caught up with on the field of Acosta Ñu, near Barrero Grande (today Eusébio Ayala)."]
        ]},
        "López was retreating north, toward the Cordillera. His army no longer had enough adults, and the government recruited whoever was left: boys, old men and the wounded. The Paraguayans call the battle Acosta Ñu ('Acosta's field'). The Brazilians call it Campo Grande."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Gastão de Orléans, Count of Eu, 27 years old" },
        "Born in France, he was married to Princess Isabel, heir to the Brazilian throne. He took over the war when it was already won, and returned to court with the nickname 'decorative marshal'. His goal was to end the resistance and capture López. He is also the man on whom the gravest accusations of this battle fall, which we will see in the last chapter.",
        { h: "Bernardino Caballero, 30 years old" },
        "A Paraguayan officer trusted by López, he commanded the rear guard. He escaped from the battle, was arrested in April 1870, released in 1871 and rose to be president of Paraguay (1880–1886). He helped found the Colorado Party, in 1887.",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "On the allied side, Colonel Manuel Deodoro da Fonseca, who would be the first president of the Republic of Brazil, was among those who led the infantry attack. Among the Argentines, who were few, Colonel Luis María Campos commanded a unit that joined the cavalry. The Viscount of Taunay, a young officer on the Count of Eu's staff, was there and left the best-known account from the Brazilian side."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The allies had about 20,000 men, the vast majority of them Brazilian, divided into two army corps, with infantry, artillery and a cavalry division.",
        "The Paraguayan side is more uncertain. Sources speak of 3,500 to 6,000 men, and the most cited range is 3,500 to 4,500. A good part were boys between 9 and 15 years old, along with old men and wounded, with some adult veterans. According to the most common account, they had eight guns.",
        { note: "The boys' ages vary by source. Some texts say 12 to 15 years old; others, children of 9 and 10. According to tradition, Caballero had false beards put on the youngest so that they would look like adults from a distance. The documents of the time do not confirm this, and the story is more a symbol than a proven fact." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open plain of about 12 km², near the meeting of two [[arroyos|arroio]], the Yukyry and the Piribebuy. The ground was good for the Brazilian cavalry, but the passage narrowed where the two creeks came close together, and that changed the order of the attack.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "The Brazilian cavalry went ahead to close the road at Barrero Grande, and the 2nd Corps followed to reinforce it. In the morning, at about 8:30, the fighting began. Since the cavalry could not advance through the narrow passage, it was the infantry of the 1st Corps that opened the attack.",
        "The Paraguayans fought and, after the first clashes, fell back to the other side of the Yukyry, where they had the eight guns and the protection of the bank. They set fire to the dry grass to hide their movements with the smoke.",
        "The allies' first attempt to cross was repulsed. The Count of Eu then ordered the artillery to fire, and the Paraguayan casualties were heavy. That opened the way: the cavalry crossed the creek and attacked the flank. The Paraguayans formed a [[square|quadrado]] with [[bayonets|baioneta]], and even so they suffered.",
        "Meanwhile, the 2nd Corps reached the Paraguayan [[rear|retaguarda]] and closed the exit. The allied infantry attacked again, with bayonets, and took the positions and the eight guns. Caballero fled with a small group. The battle lasted about eight hours."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Paraguayan losses are not known precisely. The most used estimate, by Hooker and Esposito, is about 3,500: 2,000 dead and 1,500 wounded or captured. Other sources speak of 2,000 dead and 1,200 prisoners, and some speak of 3,300 dead. Among the allies, fewer than 50 to 182 dead and 259 to 431 wounded, depending on the author.",
        "It was the last great battle. López still fled for six more months and died at Cerro Corá, on 1 March 1870. The war left Paraguay devastated: the population fell sharply, but estimates of how many died vary considerably among historians.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In Paraguay, 16 August has been Children's Day since 1948, in memory of the boys of Acosta Ñu. The battle became a symbol of what a long war does to a country, and the painting Battle of Campo Grande, by Pedro Américo, shows the Brazilian side of the same day.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The fire: the dry grass caught fire, and many wounded died burned. The historian Julio José Chiavenatto, in 1979, accused the Count of Eu of ordering the field burned on purpose. Taunay, who was there, wrote that the Paraguayans themselves lit the fire to hide their movements, and that it got out of control. Later historians, such as Francisco Doratioto, reject the accusation. What remains is the fact that the fire existed and killed." },
        { note: "The hospital: it is said that a hospital with wounded Paraguayans was set on fire by order of the Count of Eu. More recent historians think it more likely that it caught fire during the allied bombardment, at the start of the battle." },
        { note: "The numbers and the intent: it is debated how much of the story of children clinging to the legs of Brazilian soldiers comes from reliable sources. Part of it comes from Chiavenatto, and there is no independent confirmation. There are also those who warn that exalting the boy soldiers may end up justifying their recruitment." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  sedan: {
    war: "Franco-Prussian War (1870–1871)",
    hook: "In two days, an emperor was captured along with an entire army, and an empire ended.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1870, the France of Napoleon III was the greatest military power in Europe, at least in its own opinion. The Prussia of William I and Chancellor Bismarck came from two victorious wars, against Denmark and Austria, and already led the [[North German Confederation|confederação do norte da alemanha]]. What was missing was the union of the southern states, and Bismarck knew that nothing unites like a common enemy.",
        "The spark was the candidacy of a German prince for the throne of Spain, which France saw as encirclement. Bismarck edited the Ems telegram in such a way that it seemed the king had insulted the French ambassador, and France, on 19 July 1870, declared war. The German mobilization, carried out by railway and with printed timetables, was much faster than the French one.",
        { h: "The road to Sedan" },
        { timeline: [
          ["19 July 1870", "France declares war on Prussia."],
          ["18 August", "At Gravelotte, Marshal Bazaine is pushed into Metz with the Army of the Rhine. About 168,000 Germans surround the city."],
          ["Mid-August", "The Army of Châlons is formed, under Mac Mahon, to relieve Bazaine at Metz."],
          ["30 August", "At Beaumont, Mac Mahon loses about 7,500 men and 40 guns. He gives up going to Metz and falls back on Sedan."],
          ["31 August", "The French army reaches Sedan, tired and short of ammunition, and Moltke begins to close the encirclement."]
        ]},
        "Mac Mahon marched northeast, hugging the Belgian border, to then turn south and reach Bazaine. Moltke took advantage: he left two armies surrounding Metz and sent two others against Mac Mahon."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Helmuth von Moltke, 69 years old" },
        "Chief of the Prussian general staff, he was the planner of the victory. His idea was simple: use the railways to bring the armies quickly and shut the enemy in a pocket, instead of pushing him back head-on. According to Howard, on seeing the encirclement complete he is said to have remarked that now they had them in a mousetrap.",
        { h: "Napoleon III, 62 years old" },
        "Nephew of Napoleon Bonaparte, he seized power in 1851 and was proclaimed emperor in 1852. He accompanied the army, but did not actually command: command belonged to Mac Mahon, and the court and the government in Paris interfered in decisions. His great concern at Sedan was to avoid more useless bloodshed.",
        { h: "Patrice de Mac Mahon, 62 years old" },
        "Marshal of France, hero of the Italian war (Magenta, 1859). He was a brave soldier, but he accepted an operation he knew to be risky and did not order trenches dug, despite General Douay's request. He was wounded in the thigh by a shell fragment, at about 7 a.m., and lost command.",
        { h: "Ducrot and Wimpffen: two commanders in two hours" },
        "Mac Mahon named Ducrot to succeed him. Ducrot saw that the army was going to be surrounded and ordered a retreat westward, to Mézières. Shortly afterward, General Wimpffen, who had just arrived from Algeria, presented a letter giving him command if Mac Mahon fell, canceled the order and ordered an attack to the east, toward Bazeilles and Metz. The two orders, in opposite directions, threw the army into disorder."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Germans had about 200,000 men, from the 3rd and 4th Armies, with 774 guns, according to Howard. The Army of Châlons had 120,000 to 130,000 men and 564 guns.",
        "The French infantry had one advantage: the [[chassepot]], with a longer range than the Prussian [[needle gun|fuzil de agulha]]. The German artillery had another, greater one: the breech-loading steel [[Krupp|krupp]] guns, more accurate than the French ones.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Sedan is an old fortress, from the 17th century, in a valley of the Meuse River, surrounded by hills and woods. To the southeast lie Bazeilles and La Moncelle. To the north and northeast, the Givonne valley and the La Garenne wood. To the northwest, the hills of Floing and Illy. Whoever controls the hills dominates the whole valley, and it was there that the Germans placed their guns.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at dawn, around 4 a.m., von der Tann's Bavarians crossed the Meuse on pontoon bridges, in the middle of the fog, and attacked Bazeilles by surprise. The French [[marine infantry|infantaria de marinha]] and troops of the 1st Corps defended the village house by house.",
        "Second, at about 7 a.m., Mac Mahon was wounded. The contradictory orders of Ducrot and Wimpffen, which we saw above, froze the army just when the Germans were moving.",
        "Third, while the French fought in the east, the Prussian 5th Corps and the 11th marched through the west, crossing the Meuse at Donchery. At about 9 a.m., together with the Saxons and the Guard, who came from the northeast, they completed the encirclement. The French were in a [[pocket|bolsão]]: surrounded by the hills, and with no road out.",
        "Fourth, the German artillery, with its 774 guns, pounded from above and from afar. At about 1 p.m., Wimpffen tried a breakout to the southeast, which failed, and the German 22nd Division turned Douay's left flank.",
        "Fifth, General Margueritte launched the cavalry, among them the [[Chasseurs d'Afrique|caçadores da áfrica]], against the German lines at Floing, to open a way out. He was mortally wounded in the first charge, and the next two were stopped by infantry fire at about 3 p.m. According to the account, King William, who was watching from a hill, is said to have said: 'Ah, the brave fellows!'. There were 791 French casualties in those charges.",
        "Sixth, between 2 and 2:30 p.m., the German infantry reached the Calvary of Illy, and the Guard took the La Garenne wood, where the French surrendered. The troops crowded into the town, and the Germans bombarded the citadel. Napoleon III ordered the white flag raised; a general took it down, and he ordered it put up again. Wimpffen, who refused to surrender, still attempted a last attack, which failed."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "In the late afternoon, around 6 p.m., Napoleon III sent King William, by General Reille, a letter: he said that, since he had not managed to die at the head of his troops, he was handing over his sword. Bismarck demanded unconditional capitulation, and the document was signed by Wimpffen and Moltke on 2 September, at about noon.",
        "Losses, according to Clodfelter: on the German side, about 9,860 (1,310 dead, 6,443 wounded, 2,107 missing). On the French side, 3,220 dead, 14,811 wounded and about 104,000 prisoners, plus 558 guns. Sources differ on the number of prisoners, from 80,000 to 104,000. The prisoners spent days on a peninsula of the Meuse, the 'camp of misery', in the rain and with little food.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "On 4 September, Paris proclaimed the republic, and the Second Empire ended. France still fought on for five more months: the Government of National Defense refused to cede Alsace and Lorraine, Bazaine's army surrendered at Metz, and Paris was besieged and surrendered in January 1871. In the same month, on 18 January, the German Empire was proclaimed in the Palace of Versailles.",
        "Napoleon III was taken to the castle of Wilhelmshöhe, near Kassel, in Germany. Sedan is cited as a classic example of a battle of annihilation: the enemy surrounded, with no way out, and forced to surrender with the entire army.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The prisoners: historians give different numbers, from about 80,000 to 104,000, and the sources we consulted do not explain the difference. We use the whole range." },
        { note: "Sedantag: in Germany, 2 September was celebrated as a holiday, Sedan Day, until 1919." },
        { note: "It is said that Empress Eugénie, who governed in Paris as regent, on learning of the surrender asked why he had not killed himself." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  littlebighorn: {
    war: "Great Sioux War (1876–1877)",
    hook: "In less than an hour, the commander of a cavalry regiment and about 210 men were killed by warriors the army did not know existed.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "By the Treaty of Fort Laramie, of 1868, the government of the United States guaranteed the [[Lakota|lakota]] the [[Black Hills|colinas negras]], in present-day South Dakota, as exclusive territory. In 1874, an expedition commanded by Custer found gold there. The region filled with prospectors, and the government, instead of expelling them, decided to buy the land, and the Lakota refused to sell.",
        "At the end of 1875, Washington ordered the Lakota and their allies to report to the reservations by 31 January 1876, or they would be considered hostile. Many groups were far away, and winter made the journey difficult. In March, the army began the campaign.",
        { h: "The road to the Little Bighorn" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Early 1876", "The army plans three columns to surround the Lakota and Cheyenne in the Yellowstone valley."],
          ["17 June", "General Crook's column is stopped at the Rosebud and turns back."],
          ["22 June", "General Terry sends Custer and the 7th Cavalry to follow the Indians' trail, while he and Gibbon arrive from the north."],
          ["25 June", "Custer finds the village and attacks."],
          ["26 and 27 June", "The survivors surrounded on the hill hold out until Terry arrives."]
        ]},
        "The army estimated 800 to 1,500 warriors. It did not know that, that summer, many Indians from the reservations had left to join Sitting Bull's camp, which became enormous."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "George Armstrong Custer, 36 years old" },
        "He was a young general in the Civil War and became famous for cavalry charges. In 1876, he was a lieutenant colonel commanding the 7th Cavalry, and had a reputation for being brave and stubborn. He refused the Gatling guns offered to him, which would have slowed the march, according to the most cited explanation.",
        { h: "Sitting Bull, Crazy Horse and Gall" },
        "Sitting Bull, spiritual leader of the Hunkpapa, had had a vision during the [[Sun Dance|dança do sol]], weeks earlier: soldiers falling upside down into the camp. That gave courage to the people. Crazy Horse, of the Oglala, and Gall, of the Hunkpapa, were warriors who led in the field. They rejected life on the reservation and wanted the army to leave them in peace.",
        { h: "Reno and Benteen" },
        "Major Marcus Reno and Captain Frederick Benteen were Custer's officers. Reno led the attack on the valley and, because of his retreat, was criticized for the rest of his life. Benteen, who was sent to scout the southwest, did not hurry to reinforce Custer, and was also criticized afterward."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The 7th Cavalry had between 600 and 700 men, divided into twelve [[companies|companhia]], plus Crow and Arikara [[scouts|batedores]]. Before the battle, Custer divided the regiment: one part of about 125 men stayed with the [[mule train|comboio de mulas]], 125 with Benteen, 140 with Reno and about 210 with himself. The numbers vary slightly depending on the author.",
        "Modern historians estimate the village at about 8,000 people and 1,500 to 1,800 warriors, figures adopted by the US National Park Service. Other estimates run from 1,100 to 2,500. They were Lakota, Northern Cheyenne and some Arapaho.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The valley of the Little Bighorn River, in present-day Montana, with the village along the bank. On the east side of the river ran hills and ravines. The valley lay within the Crow reservation, and the Lakota were there without their permission.",
        { h: "25 June" },
        "In the morning, the scouts sighted the village from a lookout, the Crow's Nest. Custer wanted to attack at dawn the next day, but learned that his trail had been discovered, and feared the village would scatter. He decided to attack at once.",
        "At about noon, he divided the regiment. Benteen was sent to the southwest. Reno was ordered to attack the village through the valley, with the promise of support. Custer went on with his five companies along a line of hills east of the river.",
        "At about 3:10 p.m., Reno crossed the river and attacked the southern end of the village. He met more warriors than he expected and stopped. He fell back into a wood at the river's edge, and from there attempted a desperate flight across the ford, in which he lost many men, to the hills on the other side, where Benteen found him. The troops fortified themselves on what is now called Reno Hill.",
        "Custer went north, and his movements are the greatest mystery of the battle, because no one from his column survived. The most accepted version says he went down to the ravine known as Medicine Tail Coulee, went back up to the hills and was surrounded at positions now called Calhoun Hill and Last Stand Hill. The warriors attacked from several sides. Crazy Horse, according to Lakota accounts, led the final charge.",
        "Lakota accounts say the fight against Custer lasted less than half an hour. Most historians speak of up to an hour. Archaeology, with the cartridge cases found on the field, suggests that the end came in a single charge that overwhelmed the remaining soldiers, and not in a long siege.",
        { h: "26 and 27 June" },
        "Reno and Benteen defended themselves on the hill throughout the 26th. At night, the village broke up and began to move south. On the morning of the 27th, the troops of Terry and Gibbon arrived, and found the bodies of Custer and his men."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "About 268 soldiers and scouts died on the American side, and 49 to 55 were wounded. Among the dead were Custer, two of his brothers, a nephew and a brother-in-law. Lakota and Cheyenne losses are uncertain: estimates run from 31 to 100 dead and up to 160 wounded.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the greatest Indian victory against the American army, but it came at a high price. The news reached the rest of the country in early July, around the centennial of independence, and public opinion demanded revenge. Congress approved, in 1877, the seizure of the Black Hills. The Lakota were hunted down: Crazy Horse surrendered in 1877 and was killed months later; Sitting Bull fled to Canada, returned in 1881 and was killed in 1890.",
        "In 1980, the US Supreme Court ruled that the Black Hills had been taken unlawfully, and ordered compensation paid. The Lakota refused to accept the money: they want the land back.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The myth: Custer's widow, Libbie, spent decades building the image of a hero who died surrounded, never having retreated. From 1934, with a critical biography, the image began to change." },
        { note: "The debate: historians still argue over Custer's decisions, the conduct of Reno and Benteen, the refused Gatlings and the reliability of witnesses such as the scout Curley. The cartridge cases, found by the hundreds on the field, helped reconstruct the movements of both sides." },
        { note: "The name: the Lakota call the river the Greasy Grass. The Americans called it the Little Bighorn. The battle is known by both names." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  isandlwana: {
    war: "Anglo-Zulu War (1879)",
    hook: "An army of spears against modern rifles, and the worst defeat the British Empire suffered at the hands of an African people.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Zulu kingdom was the greatest military power in southern Africa. Its king, Cetshwayo, commanded an army of men who spent years serving in regiments organized by age. The British, who dominated the Cape and Natal, wanted a confederation of the whole region under their control, and the Zulu kingdom was the obstacle.",
        "High Commissioner Bartle Frere sent Cetshwayo, in December 1878, an ultimatum with demands the king could not meet: disband the army and accept a British resident. Without waiting for a reply from London, he ordered the invasion. The invasion began on 11 January 1879.",
        { h: "The road to Isandlwana" },
        { timeline: [
          ["11 December 1878", "Frere presents the ultimatum to Cetshwayo, with a deadline of 30 days."],
          ["11 January 1879", "The main column, under Lord Chelmsford, crosses the Buffalo River at Rorke's Drift and enters Zululand."],
          ["20 January", "The column camps at the foot of Isandlwana mountain, without digging trenches or forming a wagon barricade."],
          ["22 January", "Chelmsford leaves with half the force in pursuit of the Zulu army. The camp is attacked."]
        ]},
        "Chelmsford divided the forces into columns. The main one, with about 1,800 men, advanced slowly, with wagons and provisions, and camped in the valley. There were no trenches and no laager, the circle of wagons that the Boers used in hostile territory."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Lord Chelmsford" },
        "Frederic Thesiger, 2nd Baron Chelmsford, was the British commander in chief. Experienced in wars in India and Ethiopia, he underestimated the Zulus, which historians cite among the causes of the disaster. He divided his men and, on the morning of the battle, took about half the infantry away from the camp, after an enemy he never found.",
        { h: "Henry Pulleine and Anthony Durnford" },
        "Lieutenant Colonel Pulleine was left in command of the camp, with about 1,300 men. Colonel Durnford arrived at about 10:30 with some 500 men of Natal's cavalry and native infantry, plus [[rocket|foguetes]] launchers. He was senior to Pulleine, but did not change his plans. Durnford died in the battle, and Chelmsford tried to blame him afterward, without proof.",
        { h: "Ntshingwayo and Mavumengwana" },
        "The Zulu army was commanded in the field by Ntshingwayo kaMahole and Mavumengwana kaNdlela, and not by the king, who stayed in his capital. Dabulamanzi, the king's brother, commanded one of the regiments. The plan was to attack on 23 January, and the Zulus were waiting hidden in a valley when they were discovered a day earlier."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British column had about 1,800 men: some 700 British (mostly of the 24th Regiment of Foot), 2 seven-pounder guns, and hundreds of colonial soldiers and the Natal Native Contingent, few of whom had firearms. The British used the [[Martini-Henry|martini-henry]].",
        "The Zulu army totaled 20,000 to 25,000 men, of whom 10,000 to 15,000 took part in the fighting. The main weapon was the [[assegai|assegai]] and a hide shield. Few had firearms.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open plain, at the foot of Isandlwana, an isolated mountain. The camp lay beneath the mountain. To the north were the Nqutu plateau and the hidden Ngwebeni valley, where the Zulu army waited, about 11 km from the camp, it is calculated, although the exact location is disputed.",
        { h: "The battle in eight movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at about 11 a.m., a patrol of Lieutenant Raw's, chasing some Zulus, discovered the whole army sitting in silence in the valley. On finding themselves discovered, the Zulus rose and advanced at a run. They formed the [[buffalo horns formation|chifres do búfalo]] on the move, even with the order reversed.",
        "Second, Pulleine ordered one company of the 24th, and then all six, to form a [[firing line|linha de fogo]] in front of the camp. Durnford went out on the right to meet the Zulu chest.",
        "Third, the rifle volleys and the cannon fire held the Zulu center for about an hour, until shortly after noon. The rocket launchers were overrun early, after three shots.",
        "Fourth, the Zulus' left horn kept advancing to envelop the British right, without being stopped.",
        "Fifth, Durnford's men fell back to a [[donga|donga]] and then, running out of ammunition, abandoned the position. The British right wing was exposed.",
        "Sixth, Pulleine ordered a retreat to the camp. The line, stretched too thin and far from the ammunition boxes, broke. According to a Zulu tradition, an older warrior exposed himself to the fire to encourage the others, and the regiments took heart and attacked.",
        "Seventh, the retreat turned into many isolated fights, in small groups. A group of some 150 was found afterward at a single spot. At about 2:30 p.m., when a solar eclipse occurred, the last groups were still resisting.",
        "Eighth, the survivors fled toward the Buffalo River, along the path that became known as Fugitives' Drift. Many were overtaken and killed. At about 3:30 p.m., Chelmsford realized the camp had fallen, and only got there at sunset, after the battle was over."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "More than 1,300 men died on the British and colonial side, among them 52 officers and 690 regular British soldiers. The Zulus took two guns, about a thousand rifles, 400,000 cartridges and almost all the wagons. Only five imperial officers survived.",
        "Zulu losses were never counted. Estimates run from 1,000 to 3,000 dead, and the most cited fall between 1,500 and 2,000.",
        "In the late afternoon, part of the Zulu army attacked the post at Rorke's Drift, where about 150 British held out. Eleven of them received the [[Victoria Cross|cruz vitória]], a record for a single action.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Great Britain was shocked. It sent many more troops, and six months later, at Ulundi, it destroyed Zulu power. But Isandlwana showed that the empire was vulnerable and that a well-led native army could beat it in the open field.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The ammunition: Lieutenant Smith-Dorrien, a survivor, blamed ammunition boxes closed with rusty screws and the lack of screwdrivers. Other historians, such as Ian Knight, think the main problem was the overstretched line, far from the boxes. Durnford's men probably ran out of bullets before the others." },
        { note: "The blame: Chelmsford blamed Durnford, but General Wolseley and later historians rejected this. Lock and Quantrill put most of the responsibility on Chelmsford himself, for dividing the force and not protecting the camp." },
        { note: "The colours: Lieutenant Teignmouth Melvill escaped on horseback with the regimental colour, and Lieutenant Coghill went to help him. Both died after crossing the river and received the [[Victoria Cross|cruz vitória]] in 1907, posthumously. Wolseley disapproved of officers fleeing on horseback while the men died on foot." },
        { note: "The Zulus had been ordered to spare civilians in black coats, which is why some officers in dark uniforms escaped." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  angamos: {
    war: "War of the Pacific (1879–1884)",
    hook: "A single Peruvian ship held off the Chilean navy for six months, until the day the admiral who commanded it died in the conning tower.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The [[War of the Pacific|guerra do pacífico]] began because of the Atacama Desert and [[saltpeter|salitre]]. In 1879, Bolivia raised a tax on a Chilean saltpeter company in Antofagasta. Chile occupied the port in February, and Peru, which had a secret treaty with Bolivia, entered the war. Since the desert had almost no roads, whoever controlled the sea would control the war.",
        "On 21 May 1879, at Iquique, the Peruvian monitor Huáscar sank the old Chilean corvette Esmeralda, and her captain, Arturo Prat, died boarding the Huáscar. The Huáscar's commander, Miguel Grau, ordered the shipwrecked rescued. The same day, the other great Peruvian ship, the Independencia, ran aground and was lost chasing the little Covadonga. The Huáscar was left.",
        { h: "The road to Angamos" },
        { timeline: [
          ["21 May 1879", "Combat of Iquique: the Huáscar sinks the Esmeralda."],
          ["May to October", "The Huáscar attacks ports and convoys, and sinks or captures 14 transports."],
          ["5 August", "The Chilean admiral Williams Rebolledo resigns, and Galvarino Riveros takes over the fleet."],
          ["Early October", "Riveros sails with the fleet to hunt the Huáscar, divided into two divisions."],
          ["8 October", "Combat of Angamos."]
        ]},
        "For six months, Grau made the Chilean navy look useless. Chile could not carry troops by sea while the Huáscar was at large."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Miguel Grau Seminario" },
        "A Peruvian admiral, born in Piura. Careful and gentlemanly, he treated enemy prisoners and shipwrecked men with respect, and for that became known as the 'Gentleman of the Seas', even among the Chileans. He avoided battle with the whole Chilean fleet and attacked where it was not.",
        { h: "Galvarino Riveros and Juan José Latorre" },
        "Riveros was the Chilean commodore and commanded the fleet. Latorre commanded the Almirante Cochrane, one of the two ironclads, and led the faster division. It was he who did the heaviest work at Angamos.",
        { h: "The Peruvian officers" },
        "Elías Aguirre was the captain who succeeded Grau in command of the Huáscar. Diego Ferré was the admiral's aide. Pedro Gárezon was the lieutenant who took over when Aguirre died."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Peru had two vessels: the Huáscar, an iron-hulled [[monitor|monitor]], built in England in 1865, with a single [[armored turret|torre blindada]] with two 300-pounder guns, and the faster [[corvette|corveta]] Unión. Chile had six: two modern [[ironclads|encouraçado]], the Blanco Encalada and the Almirante Cochrane, the corvette O'Higgins, the little Covadonga and two armed transports, the Loa and the Matías Cousiño.",
        { h: "The place" },
        "Open sea, off Angamos Point, on the coast of the Atacama Desert, near the Mejillones peninsula. It was then Bolivian coast and is Chilean today.",
        { h: "8 October 1879" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, Riveros divided the fleet. The first division, with the Blanco Encalada, the Covadonga and the Matías Cousiño, went closer to the coast. The second, Latorre's, with the Cochrane, the O'Higgins and the Loa, went about 20 miles out to sea, to close the northern route.",
        "Second, at about 7:15 a.m., the Huáscar and the Unión, coming from the south, sighted the smoke of Latorre's ships. They changed course two or three times. The Unión, faster, fled north, and the Huáscar stayed to fight. The O'Higgins and the Loa went after the Unión and did not catch her.",
        "Third, at about 9:20 a.m., at some 3,000 meters, the Huáscar fired, and Latorre, without replying, sailed straight at her, as if to ram. The Cochrane only replied when she was about 2,200 meters away. Then the distance dropped to about 450 meters.",
        "Fourth, the Cochrane's first shot went through the Huáscar's turret and wounded the gun crews. Another cut the rudder chain, and the Peruvians had to improvise an emergency rudder.",
        "Fifth, at about 10 a.m. (some sources say 9:45), a shell struck the conning tower and killed Grau and Ferré. Aguirre took over. Shortly afterward, the Huáscar lowered her flag, and Latorre ordered the firing stopped, but someone hoisted it again.",
        "Sixth, at 10:22, the Blanco Encalada entered the fight. The two ironclads tried to ram the Huáscar with the [[ram|esporão]], and neither managed it. The ships passed about 5 meters from each other.",
        "Seventh, a shell in the turret killed everyone in it, including Aguirre. Lieutenant Gárezon took over and ordered the condenser valves opened, to sink the ship rather than hand her over.",
        "Eighth, at 10:55, the Peruvian flag fell again. The Chileans say it was lowered; Gárezon said the flag's halyard was cut by a shot, and that the ship never surrendered. At 11:08, between 14 and 20 Chilean sailors went aboard without resistance, closed the leaks and put out the fires."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Chile had 1 dead and 9 wounded. Peruvian losses vary by source: about 31 to 33 dead and 144 to 162 prisoners. The Huáscar was captured, repaired and served in the Chilean navy.",
        "Without the Huáscar, Chile controlled the sea. A few weeks later, in November 1879, it landed at Pisagua and began the invasion of Peru, which ended with the taking of Lima, in January 1881.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Chilean historian Gonzalo Bulnes wrote that the war was decided at Angamos. Grau became Peru's greatest naval hero, and 8 October is a holiday in the country. The Huáscar exists to this day, as a museum ship in Talcahuano, in Chile.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The flag: the Chileans say the Huáscar surrendered by lowering the flag. Lieutenant Gárezon said it was knocked down by a shot and that he was sinking the ship, that is, that he never surrendered. The question is one of honor and remains in dispute." },
        { note: "The numbers: one source gives 31 Peruvian dead, 4 missing and 162 prisoners; another gives 33 dead, 27 wounded and 144 prisoners. The times also vary by a few minutes, depending on the account." },
        { note: "Commander Latorre's official report is the most detailed Chilean source and cites five 300-pounder shells from the Huáscar in the Cochrane's hull. It does not mention Grau's death. The details about the dead officers come from other sources." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  ulundi: {
    war: "Anglo-Zulu War (1879)",
    hook: "Six months after Isandlwana, the British learned the lesson: a great square, cannons and machine guns on open ground.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Isandlwana, in January 1879, the British Empire sent reinforcements: by summer, about 17,000 British soldiers and 7,000 Africans were in southern Africa. King Cetshwayo tried to negotiate peace, but Chelmsford would not. He knew General Garnet Wolseley was on his way to replace him, and wanted a victory first.",
        "The column advanced slowly, building forts and camps at each stage, so as not to repeat Isandlwana. On 1 June, the Prince Imperial Eugène, exiled son of Napoleon III, who served as a volunteer in the British army, was killed on a patrol, which caused an uproar in Europe.",
        { h: "The road to Ulundi" },
        { timeline: [
          ["22 January 1879", "Isandlwana: the Zulu army destroys the British column."],
          ["1 June", "The Prince Imperial is killed by Zulus on a patrol."],
          ["3 July", "Redvers Buller's cavalry is attacked near the Mbilane stream."],
          ["4 July", "Chelmsford advances on Ulundi, the king's capital, in square formation."]
        ]},
        "Cetshwayo kept sending envoys and peace gifts, but a Zulu regiment did not let the gifts through."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Lord Chelmsford" },
        "The man of Isandlwana came back to fix the mistake. He wanted to recover his reputation before being replaced by Wolseley, and so he ignored his rival's orders and the peace offers. This time, he advanced with the force united and in closed formation. He was rewarded with the Knight Grand Cross of the Order of the Bath, but never again commanded in the field.",
        { h: "Cetshwayo and his army" },
        "Cetshwayo, king of the Zulus, tried for peace to the end. The Zulu army at Ulundi was commanded in the field by Ziwedu kaMpande, a son of King Mpande, as was Cetshwayo himself. The Zulus attacked, as at Isandlwana, in the [[buffalo horns|chifres do búfalo]] formation."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British had about 4,200 British soldiers and 1,000 African auxiliaries, with 10 guns and 2 [[Gatling guns|metralhadora gatling]]. The Zulus numbered 12,000 to 15,000.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open plain beside the White Mfolozi River, near Ulundi, the royal capital, also known as kwaNodwengu. It was the kind of open ground Chelmsford wanted, where his guns and rifles reached far.",
        { h: "The battle in seven movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, on the morning of 4 July, Chelmsford crossed the river with most of the force, carrying only ammunition and water. He left the wagons on the other side.",
        "Second, he formed a great hollow [[square|quadrado]], with no barricade or trench, with the cavalry covering the sides and the rear, and the guns and the Gatlings inside it. They say he wanted to prove that his soldiers could win in the open field.",
        "Third, the Zulus came out and attacked the square from all sides, shortly before 9 a.m., in horseshoe formation. The horsemen entered the square when the attack began.",
        "Fourth, the fire of the rifles, the guns and the Gatlings broke the charges. No warrior got within 30 yards (about 27 meters) of the British line.",
        "Fifth, about half an hour later, the Zulus hesitated and began to fall back.",
        "Sixth, the 17th Lancers rode out of the back of the square and attacked those who were left. The charge broke the formations that remained.",
        "Seventh, the Zulus fled, pursued by the lancers and the irregular cavalry. The British burned the royal kraal."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "British losses were small: 13 to 18 dead and 69 to 89 wounded, depending on the source. The Zulus' are uncertain: about 473 dead counted on the field, more than a thousand wounded, and about 1,500 casualties in all, according to other sources.",
        "The royal kraal was burned, and the Zulu chiefs began to surrender. Cetshwayo fled and was captured on 28 August 1879, and sent into exile.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Wolseley divided Zululand into thirteen chiefdoms, given to chiefs allied with the British. Shaka's dynasty lost power. Cetshwayo returned in 1883 to a reduced territory and died in 1884. The independent Zulu kingdom was over.",
        "Ulundi is usually cited in contrast with Isandlwana: a united force, in closed formation and with concentrated fire, against a larger force that had to attack from close range.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The losses: the sources do not coincide. One account gives 3 officers and 79 British soldiers dead or wounded; Wikipedia gives 13 to 18 dead and 69 to 89 wounded. No one counted the Zulu dead carefully." },
        { note: "Chelmsford received a decoration for Ulundi, but was criticized by an inquiry of the Horse Guards, the command of the British army, for the way he conducted the war." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

